{"step_index":142,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-25T13:09:32Z","content":"I see the problem! The browser opened but the URL got broken by Windows — it stripped the login parameters. Let me fix this with an HTML redirect file instead:","thinking":"The screenshot shows `auth.nexlev.io` returning \"404 Not Found\". The browser DID open, but the auth URL is returning a 404. This means the authorization endpoint `https://auth.nexlev.io/authorize` with the parameters might not be working correctly, or the URL was malformed when opened.\n\nThe issue is likely that the URL got truncated or malformed when opened via `cmd /c start`. The `start` command on Windows can have issues with long URLs that contain special characters like `&`.\n\nLet me kill the current process and try a different approach. Instead of using the OAuth flow directly, let me try to:\n\n1. Check the task log to see what URL was generated\n2. Maybe write the URL to an HTML file and open that instead, or try a different method to open the browser\n\nLooking at the screenshot again, the address bar only shows `auth.nexlev.io` with no path or query string at all, which suggests the `cmd /c start` command stripped off everything after the domain rather than the endpoint itself being broken.\n\nThis is a known Windows quirk where `start` treats `&` as a command separator, so anything after the first `&` in the URL gets cut off. I'm considering alternatives: writing an HTML redirect file and opening that, using PowerShell's Start-Process with proper quoting, or rethinking the whole OAuth approach entirely -- maybe having a browser-based JavaScript flow that surfaces the token for the user to copy and paste back to me.","tool_calls":[{"name":"manage_task","args":{"Action":"\"kill\"","TaskId":"\"9ecf2b20-4f14-47c7-887d-090725d6fd1c/task-140\"","toolAction":"\"Killing broken process\"","toolSummary":"\"Kill old task\""}}]}
